## Remediation

SQL injection is a technique where malicious SQL statements are inserted into application-generated queries to manipulate the database. This can lead to unauthorized data access, data loss, and even full system compromise. SQL injection attacks are facilitated when user input is improperly sanitized and concatenated into SQL queries.

To remediate SQL injection vulnerabilities, user input should never be directly concatenated into SQL queries. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements provided by the database library. Sequelize supports parameterized queries through the `replacements` option, which safely escapes user input.

#### Code examples

```javascript
// VULNERABLE CODE - User input is concatenated directly into the SQL query
const userInput = req.query.name;
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '${userInput}'`;
sequelize.query(query);
```

```javascript
// SECURE CODE - User input is passed as a replacement parameter
const userInput = req.query.name;
const query = 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?';
sequelize.query(query, { replacements: [userInput] });
```

#### Additional recommendations
- Follow the principle of least privilege and only grant the minimum required database permissions.
- Implement input validation to filter out malicious input before using it in queries.
- Keep software up-to-date and apply security patches promptly.
- Adhere to the OWASP Top 10 and other security best practices.
- Consider using an Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) library like Sequelize, which provides built-in protection against SQL injection when used correctly.

