## Remediation

The libxml library processes user-input with the `noent` attribute set to `true`, which can lead to being vulnerable to XML External Entities (XXE) type attacks. XXE vulnerabilities allow an attacker to interfere with an application's processing of XML data, potentially leading to unauthorized access, data leaks, denial of service, and other severe impacts.

To fix this issue, you should set the `noent` option to `false` when using the libxml library to parse XML data. This disables the external entity expansion feature, preventing XXE attacks. Additionally, you should validate and sanitize all user input to prevent injection of malicious XML content.

#### Code examples

```javascript
// VULNERABLE CODE - `noent` is set to `true`, allowing XXE attacks
const libxml = require('libxmljs');
const userInput = req.body.xmlData;
const parsedXml = libxml.parseXmlString(userInput, { noent: true });
```

```javascript
// SECURE CODE - `noent` is set to `false`, disabling external entity expansion
const libxml = require('libxmljs');
const userInput = req.body.xmlData;
const sanitizedInput = sanitizeXml(userInput); // Sanitize user input
const parsedXml = libxml.parseXmlString(sanitizedInput, { noent: false });
```

#### Additional recommendations
- Follow the OWASP XXE Prevention Cheat Sheet for best practices.
- Implement input validation and sanitization for all user-supplied data.
- Consider using a secure XML parser library that is not vulnerable to XXE attacks.
- Adhere to the OWASP Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS) for secure coding practices.
- If possible, avoid processing user-supplied XML data entirely and use alternative data formats.

