## Remediation

SQL injection is a technique where malicious SQL statements are inserted into application entry points, allowing attackers to view, modify, or delete data in the database. This vulnerability arises when user input is used to construct SQL queries without proper sanitization or validation, enabling attackers to execute arbitrary SQL commands on the database.

To remediate this issue, you should use parameterized queries or an object-relational mapper (ORM) instead of manually constructing SQL strings with user input. Parameterized queries separate the SQL query from the user input, preventing the input from being interpreted as part of the query. ORMs abstract the database operations, providing a secure way to interact with the database.

#### Code examples

```javascript
// VULNERABLE CODE - User input is directly concatenated into the SQL query
const userId = req.body.userId;
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${userId}`;
db.query(query, (err, results) => { ... });
```

```javascript
// SECURE CODE - Using parameterized queries
const userId = req.body.userId;
const query = 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?';
db.query(query, [userId], (err, results) => { ... });
```

#### Additional recommendations
- Follow the principle of least privilege and grant the minimum required permissions to the database user.
- Implement input validation and sanitization for all user input before using it in database queries.
- Keep your software up-to-date and apply security patches promptly.
- Adhere to the OWASP Top 10 and other security best practices.
- Consider using an ORM like Sequelize, which provides built-in protection against SQL injection.
- Implement defense-in-depth measures, such as web application firewalls (WAFs) and runtime application self-protection (RASP) tools.

